The reviewed Rust feature specs describe the reference implementation. Go v2 may
improve its validation, ownership, cancellation, and failure behavior;
accidental Rust limitations are not requirements to reproduce.

Implementation: DecodeInto is supplied by step 02 and NextInto by step 03;
final-tree release verification remains a later gate. Applications define the row representation
and may provide a reusable instance for decoding:

- Tuple destination flattening becomes explicit tuple row structs/accessors;
  dynamic member access still checks every asserted member. Preseeded map-pointer
  mechanics become explicit typed field updates, while value/null results remain.

3. **Ownership/race gate:** tests retain old pages while new ones reuse the pool;
   close rows with prefetched results; cancel before/after admission; complete a
   speculative winner while a loser write blocks; retry/reprepare; use a pager;
   close sessions with delivered leases. Custom encoder call count is one per
   Bind across all routing/retries/pages/batches. Bound.Wait must not return before
   final write or active pager reference; retained result leases must not delay it.

- Parsing a token from a string directly stores the integer, unlike `Token::new`;
  it does not normalize the minimum signed value. The source therefore does not
  support a universal assertion that every Token value is normalized.

The named subsections under Language-independent behavior are the subfeatures of
this specification: configurable fetch scope; bootstrap and control recovery;
topology/schema normalization; public metadata inspection; event scheduling; snapshot merging/publication;
host listeners; client-route/tablet coordination; schema agreement; and shutdown.
Their implementation entry points are mapped individually in the evidence table.
The invariants above constrain their interactions as well as each local behavior.

Description: validate fetch-plan/polling logic, merge precedence, schema
normalization, node reuse, and cancellation. Most are deterministic local tests;
the fixed-connection tests below use proxy-backed connection setup.
